      "heading": "What The Transport Fever 3 Campaign Covers At Launch",
      "body": "What The Transport Fever 3 Campaign Covers At Launch\n\nThe Transport Fever 3 campaign mode listed on the Steam store page puts you in charge of region-spanning infrastructure upgrades, building transport networks that evolve across different eras. Specific mission count, era milestones, and pacing details for the Transport Fever 3 campaign are not announced as of 2026-09-28. The campaign sits alongside sandbox and scenario modes, so it is one of three distinct ways to play.\n\nCampaign Mode Basics From The Steam Store Page\n\nThe Steam store page description frames the Transport Fever 3 campaign as a guided progression through connected regions rather than a single open sandbox map. You build railroads, roads, and supporting infrastructure while upgrading vehicles to keep up with shifting demand. The Steam page tags Simulation, Strategy, Transportation, Trains, City Builder, Economy, and Management, mirroring the broader Transport Fever franchise.\n\nAccording to the Steam page, the campaign takes place across multiple transport eras, which means you begin with the earliest locomotives and buses and progress to more modern equipment. Each era typically introduces new industries, cargo types, and passenger classes, so your network planning has to scale over time. The store page confirms that Transport Fever 3 launches into Early Access, which is the right context for treating the campaign as a working framework that Urban Games will expand after launch.\n\nThe campaign is also one of the three listed map modes, alongside sandbox and scenario. That distinction matters because each mode answers a different player goal: campaign is the guided progression path, sandbox is open-ended free play, and scenario is a constrained challenge. The exact split between these modes, including how long the campaign runs in real hours, is not announced as of 2026-09-28.\n\nFranchise Legacy As Sibling Disambiguation Only\n\nThe Transport Fever franchise has used region-based progression in earlier titles, but Transport Fever 3 is a new entry and not a reskin of Transport Fever 2. Players who search for the Transport Fever 3 campaign sometimes land on Transport Fever 2 campaign 3 mission 1 results because of autocomplete noise. Those mission names and chapter structures belong to Transport Fever 2 and do not describe Transport Fever 3. Use them only as a reference point for how a Transport Fever campaign can be paced, never as a spoiler for Transport Fever 3.\n\nThe franchise's earlier entries, including Train Fever and Transport Fever, share a few design instincts: regional maps, era progression, and a mix of freight and passenger demand. Those instincts are useful orientation, but the Steam store page for Transport Fever 3 is the only authoritative source for what this specific entry contains. Anything imported from a previous Transport Fever title should be treated as sibling context rather than fact.\n\nMission Count, Era Milestones, And Pacing\n\nSpecific facts such as the total mission count, exact era boundaries, and pacing milestones for the Transport Fever 3 campaign are not announced as of 2026-09-28. The Steam store page currently confirms the campaign mode exists and is part of Early Access, but it does not publish a numbered mission list. Check the official Steam store page for updates as Urban Games expands the campaign through Early Access patches.\n\nPlayers who want to keep their expectations grounded can track community walkthroughs on the Steam Community Hub once Early Access begins. The Hub is the right place to compare observed tips with the official description on the Steam store page, especially for any mission that has not been fully documented yet. Avoid fan wikis that import Transport Fever 2 mission names as if they applied to Transport Fever 3, because that import is a category error rather than helpful context.\n\nFor the broader gameplay picture, the vehicle roster hub covers trains, buses, trucks, aircraft, and ships as Steam-tagged categories that appear across campaign eras. The map modes reference hub explains how the campaign fits alongside sandbox and scenario modes, and the economy guide breaks down the financial decisions that drive each era's network growth."
